## Why This Is Asked

Explores new angles in data privacy for IoT edge-to-cloud pipelines, focusing on end-to-end data minimization, attestation, and verifiable auditability rather than generic controls.

## Key Concepts

- Edge data minimization and in-flight redaction
- Device attestation (SPIRE) and secure TEEs for inference
- Encrypted communications (mTLS) and envelope encryption (KMS)
- ABAC for per-tenant access in cloud data lakes
- Tamper-evident logging and verifiable deletion/auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: redactPII at edge before forwarding
function redactPII(record){
  if(record.email) record.email = "REDACTED";
  if(record.phone) record.phone = "REDACTED";
  if(record.ssn) record.ssn = "REDACTED";
  return record;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate end-to-end privacy guarantees (tests, metrics, and audits)?
- What failure modes would you simulate, and how would incident response orchestrate recovery?

Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design secure multi-tenant serverless with real-world constraints: isolation, attestation, key management, deployment safety, and auditability.

Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation and sandboxing
- Hardware-backed signing (HSM) and per-tenant keys
- Runtime attestation and manifest/SBOM checks
- Ephemeral credentials and mTLS
- Canary deployments, rollback, tamper-evident logs
- Key rotation and incident response

Code Example

```javascript
// Example: verify signed package manifest before deployment
const crypto = require('crypto');
const manifest = loadManifest(tenantId);
const signatureBase64 = loadSignature(tenantId);
const publicKeyPem = loadPublicKey(tenantId);

const verifier = crypto.createVerify('SHA256');
verifier.update(manifest);
verifier.end();
const ok = verifier.verify(publicKeyPem, signatureBase64, 'base64');
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate tenant keys without downtime?
- How would you test the sandbox for side-channel attacks and data exfiltration risk?

## Why This Is Asked
Understanding real-world tenant isolation, cryptographic key management, and incident response in a sandboxed multi-tenant pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation, per-tenant keys, envelope encryption, runtime attestation, SBOM, tamper-evident logs.
- Sandboxed rules using WASM with restricted APIs and strict surface area.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: hash chaining for tamper-evident logs
function hashLog(prevHash, record) {
  const json = JSON.stringify(record);
  const data = prevHash + json;
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(data).digest('hex');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test tenancy boundaries under high throughput?
- What trade-offs exist between sandbox isolation and latency?

## Why This Is Asked
This question checks practical, beginner-friendly steps to enforce strict per-tenant isolation in a real API stack, not just high-level definitions.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation
- JWT tenant_id verification
- PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS)
- Deny-by-default authorization
- Per-endpoint guardrails and logging
- CI/CD testing for multi-tenant access

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js middleware example
function tenantMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const tokenTid = req.user?.tenant_id;
  const pathTid = req.params.tenant_id || req.body.tenant_id;
  if (!pathTid || tokenTid !== pathTid) return res.status(403).send("Forbidden");
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test RLS policies in a CI pipeline?
- How would you monitor for tenant_id leakage in production?
